response:
The area of each square added at a given stage is 3 inches by 3 inches, so the area of each square is \(3^2 = 9\) square inches. At Stage 6, the area contributed by each square added at successive splits is: \[
- \text{Stage 1: } 1 \quad \text{(one square)}
- \text{Stage 2: } 2 \quad \text{(two more squares)}
- \text{Stage 3: } 4 \quad \text{(two more squares)}
- \text{Stage 4: } 8 \quad \text{(two more squares)}
- \text{Stage 5: } 16 \quad \text{(two more squares)}
- \text{Stage 6: } 32 \quad \text{(two more squares)}
\]
So, the total number of squares at Stage 6 is \(1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 + 32 = 63\). Since each square has an area of 9 square inches, the total area at Stage 6 is \(63 \times 9 = 567\) square inches.
</think> <answer>567</answer>

